+# Research consulted
+
+| Source title | URL | Consulted | Claim used |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Product Interoperability Matrix — Upgrade Path | https://interopmatrix.broadcom.com/Upgrade | 2026-08-14 | The live upgrade-path matrix was checked for the source and target product releases; the pinned snapshot supplies the deterministic transition values. |
+| Upgrade Sequence and Related Issues for VMware Cloud Foundation and VMware vSphere Foundation 9.1 | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/440630/upgrade-sequence-and-related-issues-for.html | 2026-08-14 | VCF 5.2.x estates using SDDC Manager can upgrade to 9.1 by upgrading VCF Operations first and then following the lifecycle workflows. |
+| VCF Upgrade Failure: Cannot Upgrade VCF 5.2.2 to 9.0.1 or 9.0.2 Due to NSX Back-in-Time Error (NSX 4.2.3.3) | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/430825 | 2026-08-14 | NSX 4.2.3.3 cannot take the older 9.0.1/9.0.2 builds; VCF 9.1 is the supported upgrade target that fixes the chronological build restriction. |
+| How to convert a vSAN OSA cluster to a vSAN ESA cluster | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/432894/how-to-convert-a-vsan-osa-original-stora.html | 2026-08-14 | vSAN OSA cannot be converted to ESA in place; build a certified ESA cluster, migrate and verify workloads, then decommission OSA. |
+| vSAN Health Service — Proactive Tests — Network Performance Test | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/326905 | 2026-08-14 | vSAN ESA requires at least 25 Gbps aggregate NIC bandwidth per host. |
+| VM Summary Tab shows the storage used more than actual space consumed by Guest OS | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/394723 | 2026-08-14 | For vSAN FTT=1 RAID-5, OSA 3+1 requires four hosts while ESA 2+1 requires three. |
